From 93cbc58f5193d04c940605e32bb3362abadbe837 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 04:27:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 18/20] perf(ppc): msum-based quantized kernels, threaded
 GEMVs, fused K-quants

Rework the PowerPC quantized matmul kernels (shared by AltiVec and VSX):

- Q2_K, Q3_K and Q5_K no longer dequantize the whole N x K weight matrix
  to fp32 on every call (hundreds of MB for an output projection, which
  also cannot be allocated in a 32-bit process). They get fused per-block
  kernels like Q4_K/Q6_K.
- Unsigned x signed byte dots use vec_msum (vmsummbm: 16 multiply-adds
  in one instruction, available on every AltiVec/VSX CPU) instead of
  vmulesb/vmulosb + two vsum4shs. Weights are widened to unsigned bytes
  (Q4_x/Q5_x nibbles, K-quant 2..6-bit values, I-quant grids); format
  offsets and minimums are applied once per block from the activation
  sums (Q8_K bsums, or per-block sums for the 32-element formats)
  instead of a 32-iteration scalar loop per block and weight row.
- Q5_0/Q5_1 build their fifth bit with a permute/mask instead of a
  per-element scalar loop; horizontal sums use vsumsws.
- The Q4_0/Q4_1/Q5_x/Q8_0 GEMVs ran on one thread; split the weight rows
  across the pool like the other kernels. Activations are quantized once
  per row into a 16-byte-aligned layout so their loads are plain lvx.
- Block scales are decoded with a branch-free exact fp16 conversion
  instead of std::ldexp.
- The row-wise dequant fallback (formats without a fused kernel) uses the
  vectorized dot_f32.

Kernels are checked against the scalar dequant routines; Q2_K/Q3_K/Q5_K
GEMM tests are added. The PowerPC kernel suite (396 checks) and the IQ
tests pass on a 7450 (qemu-ppc), and the sources compile for VSX.

